 <section class="three-tool-section" id="white-meaning"><h3>What are Ayyam al-Bid, and why are they called White Days?</h3>
  <p>White Days is the English name for <em>al-Ayyam al-Bid</em>, associated with the bright moonlit nights around the middle of a lunar month. They refer to the <strong>13th, 14th and 15th Hijri daytime dates</strong> often recommended for fasting, not to the 13th–15th of each Gregorian month.</p>
  <p>A Hijri date begins at sunset on the preceding civil evening. The fast itself is observed during the <em>daytime</em> of Hijri 13, 14 or 15, from true dawn to sunset. A calendar app that displays only a Gregorian date is a planning aid, not a direct measurement of lunar visibility.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="white-sunnah"><h3>What is the authentic evidence for fasting three days a month?</h3>
  <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:2449" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunan Abi Dawud 2449</a> reports encouragement to fast the thirteenth, fourteenth and fifteenth days of the month; Sunnah.com displays a Sahih grading attributed to al-Albani. A broader report in <a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:1160" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 1160</a> describes fasting three days each month without making one particular set of days the exclusive possibility.</p>
  <p>These sources should be read in their contexts. For ordinary months, the White Days pattern is an optional practice. It does not automatically override a separate prohibition, obligation, personal exemption or applicable school-specific fasting guidance.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="white-calendar"><h3>How to calculate upcoming White Days correctly</h3>
  <ol class="three-tool-steps"><li><strong>Choose a Gregorian reference date.</strong> The tool identifies its calculated Hijri month using the Umm al-Qura convention.</li><li><strong>Select 3, 6 or 12 months.</strong> You will see a complete set of days 13, 14 and 15 for each listed Hijri month.</li><li><strong>Review the first partial month.</strong> Before or on the 15th, earlier dates in that month remain visible and are labelled passed; after the 15th, the plan moves to the next Hijri month.</li><li><strong>Cross-check local dates.</strong> The optional ±1 display shows neighbouring Gregorian dates only. It is <strong>not</strong> a confirmed local sighting adjustment.</li><li><strong>Check the exceptions.</strong> Ramadan dates are labelled obligatory; Dhul Hijjah 13 is flagged as Tashreeq.</li><li><strong>Export carefully.</strong> The full CSV keeps the status of every date; the calendar file includes only future potential voluntary dates.</li></ol>
  <p>For the underlying conversion method, visit the <a href="/tools/hijri-converter/">Hijri Converter</a>. For calculated Fajr and Maghrib on an actual fasting day, use <a href="/tools/fasting-times/">Suhoor &amp; Iftar Times</a>.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="white-exceptions"><h3>White Days during Ramadan and the days of Tashreeq</h3>
  <div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>Ramadan 13, 14 and 15</h4><p>These dates occur within <strong>obligatory Ramadan fasting</strong>, addressed in <a href="https://quran.com/2/185" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:185</a>. They are not a separate set of additional voluntary fasts.</p></div><div><h4>Dhul Hijjah 13</h4><p>The 11th, 12th and 13th days of Dhul Hijjah are the <strong>days of Tashreeq</strong>. <a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:1141a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 1141a</a> describes them as days of eating and drinking. An ordinary optional White Day fast is not recommended for the 13th.</p></div></div>
  <p>The planner keeps Dhul Hijjah 14 and 15 as potential ordinary voluntary dates, but excludes the 13th from the eligible ICS download. Some Hajj-specific exceptions and individual fasting rulings require qualified religious guidance; this simple tool cannot evaluate them.</p>
  <p class="three-tool-caution">Do not use the automatically generated calendar to override an established fast, an exemption for illness or travel, or the guidance of your recognised religious authority. This is date organisation, not a personalized fiqh verdict.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="white-example"><h3>Worked example: a White Days set crossing a Gregorian week</h3>
  <div class="three-tool-example"><p>Imagine a calculated Hijri month in which the <strong>13th</strong> falls on a Gregorian <strong>Monday</strong>. The <strong>14th</strong> would normally be Tuesday and the <strong>15th</strong> Wednesday, unless a calendar adjustment is applied. If the community's month start is established one civil day later than the computational calendar, their corresponding dates may move to <strong>Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday</strong>.</p><p>These weekday examples are hypothetical; they do <strong>not</strong> designate a particular month's verified White Days. A one-day toggle in the planner only previews such a difference.</p></div>
  <p>For a reference date on the Hijri 14th, the planner still shows 13, 14 and 15; the 13th is marked as passed. For a reference after the 15th, it begins with the next Hijri month so the forward plan stays useful.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="white-export"><h3>Printing, CSV and calendar events: what is saved?</h3>
  <ul><li><strong>Print / Save PDF:</strong> prints all selected months, their three Hijri dates and special status notes.</li><li><strong>Full CSV:</strong> preserves each Hijri year, month, day, estimated Gregorian date, status (voluntary, Ramadan obligatory or Tashreeq), whether the date has passed, and any illustrative day shift.</li><li><strong>Eligible ICS:</strong> adds only upcoming potential ordinary voluntary fast dates as <strong>all-day Gregorian date markers</strong>. It excludes Ramadan and 13 Dhul Hijjah, and provides no Fajr alarm or binding fasting verdict.</li></ul>
  <p>The planner does not store a fasting-history checklist or send selected dates to an online prayer calendar. Downloads are created in the browser when you click. These are static files; update them if a local moon-sighting announcement changes a date.</p>
 </section>
